# Components

Howdy UI comes with more than fifty ready-made components: text, buttons,
form controls, cards, tables, charts, dialogs, menus, tabs and more. Each is
a function that returns a [Lustre](https://hexdocs.pm/lustre) element,
already styled, that follows whatever [theme](/docs/ui/themes) the page is
using.

To see them, browse the [component gallery](/components). Every component,
block and theme preset has a page there with its examples drawn live, the
code that drew each one, the command that copies it into your project, and
its full source.

## Using a component

Import `howdy/ui`, which re-exports every component, and call them like any
other Lustre element:

```gleam
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/button.{Outline, Primary}
import lustre/element.{text}

ui.card([], [
  ui.card_header([], [
    ui.card_title([text("Delete this order?")]),
    ui.card_description([text("This cannot be undone.")]),
  ]),
  ui.card_footer([], [
    ui.button(Outline, [], [text("Cancel")]),
    ui.button(Primary, [], [text("Delete")]),
  ]),
])
```

Render them in a [page](/docs/ui/pages) or in the view of a
[live component](/docs/ui/live-components). Both include the CSS for the
components they draw, so there is nothing else to set up.

## How they are called

Components follow Lustre's conventions, so they mix freely with elements
from `lustre/element/html`:

- A component that holds other elements takes a list of attributes and a
  list of children, as in `ui.card([], [...])`.
- A component that only holds text takes a `String`, as in `ui.h1("Orders")`
  or `ui.muted("Updated two minutes ago")`.
- A component that comes in several looks takes the variant first, as in
  `ui.button(Primary, [], [...])` or `ui.badge(Secondary, [], [...])`. The
  variants live in the component's own module, such as `howdy/ui/button`.
- Attributes come from `lustre/attribute` and events from `lustre/event`.
  Whatever you pass is added to the element beside its styling.

```gleam
import lustre/attribute
import lustre/event

ui.card([attribute.id("summary")], [ui.h2("Summary")])
ui.button(Primary, [event.on_click(Save)], [text("Save")])
```

Each component also has its own module under `howdy/ui`, such as
`howdy/ui/button` or `howdy/ui/card`. Its page in the
[gallery](/components) names the module. A few components with many
options, `calendar`, `data_table`, `chart` and `uploader`, are
[builders](#Builders) used from their own modules only.

## What there is

The [gallery](/components) groups them the same way:

- *Typography*: headings `ui.h1` to `ui.h4`, paragraphs, muted text,
  links, `prose` for rendered Markdown, and keyboard keys.
- *Actions*: [buttons](/components/button),
  [button groups](/components/button_group) and
  [toggles](/components/toggle).
- *Forms*: inputs and text areas, [fields](/components/field) with hints
  and errors, checkboxes and radios, switches, sliders, one-time code
  inputs, [selects](/components/select),
  [comboboxes and command menus](/components/command),
  [calendars and date pickers](/components/calendar), questionnaires
  that ask one question at a time, and [uploaders](/components/uploader)
  that send files straight to storage.
- *Layout*: containers, stacks and rows, [cards](/components/card), an
  application [sidebar](/components/sidebar), scroll areas, resizable
  panels and right-to-left sections.
- *Overlays*: [dialogs and sheets](/components/dialog), drawers, popovers,
  tooltips, [menus](/components/menu), context menus, menubars and hover
  cards.
- *Navigation*: [tabs](/components/tabs), accordions, pagination,
  breadcrumbs and navigation menus.
- *Data display*: tables, [data tables](/components/data_table),
  [charts](/components/chart), badges, avatars, carousels and item lists.
- *Feedback*: progress bars, empty states, [alerts](/components/alert),
  [toasts](/components/toast), skeletons and spinners.
- *Chat*: a [conversation](/components/chat) that stays on the newest
  message, and file attachments.

## Buttons

`ui.button` takes a variant, attributes and children. The variants are in
`howdy/ui/button`:

- `Primary` is the main action, filled with the primary colour. Aim for
  one per view.
- `Secondary` is a supporting action, filled with the muted colour.
- `Outline` is a supporting action, outlined.
- `Ghost` is a quiet action, such as one in a toolbar, filled only when
  hovered.
- `Link` is an action that looks like a link.
- `Danger` is a destructive action, filled with the danger colour.

`ui.sized_button` takes a size as well: `ExtraSmall`, `Small`, `Medium` or
`Large`, or `Icon`, `IconExtraSmall`, `IconSmall` or `IconLarge` for a
square button holding one icon. Give an icon button an `aria-label`.

```gleam
import howdy/ui/button.{Danger, Ghost, Icon, Primary}

ui.button(Primary, [event.on_click(Save)], [text("Save")])
ui.button(Danger, [attribute.disabled(True)], [text("Delete")])
ui.sized_button(Ghost, Icon, [attribute.aria_label("Close")], [text("×")])
```

A button does nothing on its own. That is what you want in a live view,
where `event.on_click` does the work. To submit an HTML form, use
`ui.submit_button`:

```gleam
ui.submit_button(Primary, [], [text("Sign up")])
```

Do not pass `attribute.type_("submit")` to `ui.button` instead. The button
would have two types, which a page and a live view resolve differently.

See the [button page](/components/button) for every variant and size.

## Forms

`ui.field` groups a label, a control and the text that explains or
corrects it:

```gleam
html.form([attribute.method("post"), attribute.action("/signups")], [
  ui.stack([], [
    ui.field([], [
      ui.label([attribute.for("email")], [text("Email address")]),
      ui.input([
        attribute.id("email"),
        attribute.name("email"),
        attribute.type_("email"),
        attribute.aria_describedby("email-hint"),
      ]),
      ui.field_description([attribute.id("email-hint")], [
        text("We never share it."),
      ]),
    ]),
    ui.submit_button(Primary, [], [text("Sign up")]),
  ]),
])
```

Always pair a control with a label, joined by matching `for` and `id`.
The label is what a screen reader announces, and clicking it focuses the
control.

Controls are styled by their own state. `attribute.disabled(True)` dims
them, and `attribute.aria_invalid("true")` gives them the danger colour,
so the attributes that tell assistive technology about an error also show
it:

```gleam
ui.field([], [
  ui.label([attribute.for("email")], [text("Email address")]),
  ui.input([
    attribute.id("email"),
    attribute.aria_invalid("true"),
    attribute.aria_describedby("email-error"),
  ]),
  ui.field_error([attribute.id("email-error")], [
    text("Enter an email address."),
  ]),
])
```

The other controls work the same way: `ui.textarea`, `ui.checkbox`,
`ui.radio` and `ui.choice` (a control with its label), `ui.switch`,
`ui.slider`, `ui.select` and `ui.combobox`. [Forms](/docs/controllers/forms)
covers reading and validating what the form sends.

### Uploads

`howdy/ui/uploader` is a place to drop or choose files. The browser sends
each one straight to storage, showing its progress as an attachment, and
the form posts the files' keys instead of the files:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/attachment
import howdy/ui/uploader

html.form([attribute.method("post"), attribute.action("/listings")], [
  ui.stack([], [
    uploader.new(name: "photos", start: "/listings/uploads")
      |> uploader.multiple(max_files: 8)
      |> uploader.accept(["image/png", "image/jpeg"])
      |> uploader.max_size(10_000_000)
      |> uploader.layout(attachment.Tile)
      |> uploader.view,
    ui.submit_button(Primary, [], [text("Publish")]),
  ]),
])
```

`start` is an endpoint of yours. The browser posts it each file's name,
type and size as JSON, and it answers with a key and where to send the
file, which `howdy_storage` gives you; see
[direct uploads](/docs/storage/overview#Direct-uploads). Files over the
size or of other types are refused before they are sent, the form will not
submit while one is still on its way, and `uploader.stored` shows files
saved earlier so an edit form keeps them unless they are removed. In a
live view, `live.on_values("photos", PhotosChanged)` hears the keys each
time they change.

## Layout

- `ui.container` is a centred column, at most 960 pixels wide, with padding
  at the edges. Wrap the content of a page in one.
- `ui.stack` places its children in a column with an even gap.
- `ui.row` places its children in a line with a gap, centred vertically,
  wrapping when they run out of room.
- `ui.separator` is a dividing line.
- `ui.card` is a raised surface for related content, with
  `card_header`, `card_title`, `card_description`, `card_action`,
  `card_content` and `card_footer` for its parts.

```gleam
ui.container([], [
  ui.stack([], [
    ui.h1("Dashboard"),
    ui.row([], [
      ui.card([], [ui.h3("Orders"), ui.p([text("128")])]),
      ui.card([], [ui.h3("Customers"), ui.p([text("64")])]),
    ]),
  ]),
])
```

Use `stack` and `row` for spacing rather than adding margins to the things
inside them. The gap belongs to the layout, so components stay reusable and
spacing stays even.

For a whole application, `ui.sidebar_layout` puts a collapsible
[sidebar](/components/sidebar) beside the page.

## Interactive components

Dialogs, popovers, menus, tooltips, tabs, accordions and selects are built
on what the browser already does: the `<dialog>` element, the popover API
and `<details>`. The browser keeps focus inside a modal, closes things on
Escape or a click outside, and puts focus back afterwards.

A trigger and what it opens are tied by an id. The trigger functions return
attributes, so any button can open one:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/button.{Outline, Primary}

html.div([], [
  ui.button(Outline, ui.dialog_trigger("rename"), [text("Rename")]),
  ui.dialog("rename", [], [
    ui.dialog_header([
      ui.dialog_title("rename", [text("Rename project")]),
    ]),
    ui.input([attribute.aria_label("Name")]),
    ui.dialog_footer([
      ui.button(Outline, ui.dialog_close("rename"), [text("Cancel")]),
      ui.button(Primary, ui.dialog_close("rename"), [text("Save")]),
    ]),
  ]),
])
```

Menus work the same way, with `ui.menu_trigger` and `ui.menu`:

```gleam
html.div([], [
  ui.button(Outline, ui.menu_trigger("account"), [text("Account")]),
  ui.menu("account", [], [
    ui.menu_item([event.on_click(OpenProfile)], [text("Profile")]),
    ui.menu_separator(),
    ui.menu_link("/sign-out", [], [text("Sign out")]),
  ]),
])
```

A small script, `howdy/ui/behaviour`, adds what the browser does not do:
arrow keys and typeahead in menus and selects, arrow keys between tabs, and
tooltips on hover and focus. Every page includes it, and it works inside
live views too, without a round trip to the server.

The browser keeps track of what is open and what is selected. A live view
that wants to know listens for the native events: `close` on a dialog,
`toggle` on a popover, `change` on a select, or `click` on a tab or menu
item. Keep a trigger and what it opens in the same place: both in the page,
or both in one live view.

## Builders

`calendar`, `data_table` and `chart` have many options, so they are built
up in a pipeline and drawn with `view`. Use them from their own modules:

```gleam
import gleam/int
import gleam/string
import howdy/ui/data_table

let columns = [
  data_table.column("id", "Invoice", fn(invoice) { text(invoice.id) }),
  data_table.column("customer", "Customer", fn(invoice) {
    text(invoice.customer)
  })
    |> data_table.sortable(fn(a, b) { string.compare(a.customer, b.customer) }),
  data_table.column("amount", "Amount", fn(invoice) {
    text(int.to_string(invoice.amount))
  })
    |> data_table.sortable(fn(a, b) { int.compare(a.amount, b.amount) })
    |> data_table.numeric,
]

data_table.new(columns, invoices)
|> data_table.empty([text("No invoices yet.")])
|> data_table.view
```

Sorting, selection and paging happen on the server: the table shows the
rows it is given. The [data table](/components/data_table),
[calendar](/components/calendar) and [chart](/components/chart) pages show
the rest of their options.

Charts are drawn on the server too, as SVG and HTML, and need no script.
"Show data" opens the numbers as a table, so no value depends on colour or
a mouse.

## Blocks

Blocks are whole screens and cards built from the components: an
[application shell](/components/app_shell) with a sidebar and top bar, a
[stat card](/components/stat_card), and
[sign-in](/components/sign_in) and [sign-up](/components/sign_up) screens.
Use them from `howdy/ui/blocks`, or copy one into your project to make it
your own.

## Styling another element

Each component's module exposes the class behind it, such as
`card.card_class()`. Use one to give the same look to a different
element:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/card
import lustre/element/html

html.section([ui.class(card.card_class())], [ui.h2("Summary")])
```

## Making them your own

Howdy UI does not have an option for every detail. When a component is not
quite right, copy its source into your project and change it:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui add button
```

Every page in the [gallery](/components) shows the command for that
component. [Custom components](/docs/ui/custom-components) covers copying,
keeping copies up to date, and writing components of your own.
